<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Info.plist for Saathi.app.

    The bundle's main executable is SaathiApp, the menu-bar app: LSUIElement is true, so there is
    no Dock tile, and it is what LaunchServices launches. The `saathi` CLI ships beside it at
    Contents/MacOS/saathi for the Homebrew cask's symlink onto PATH; the bundle never launches it.

    The reason any of this is a bundle at all: a command-line executable has NO main bundle, and
    macOS requires the usage-description strings below to live in the main bundle before it will
    let a process ask for the microphone or for speech recognition. Probed on the loose binary,
    the answers were:

        main bundle id:                       none — not a bundle
        NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription:  ABSENT
        NSMicrophoneUsageDescription:         ABSENT

    So the chain voice lane — which is the DEFAULT lane, the one that works with no key — cannot
    ask for what it needs unless the executable is inside a bundle.

    Launching through LaunchServices — `open Saathi.app`, or double-clicking it — is also what
    makes a permission prompt attributed to Saathi rather than to whatever terminal started a
    process: TCC attributes a prompt to the RESPONSIBLE process, and running the CLI binary
    straight from a shell does not get that for free.

    The strings themselves matter more here than in most apps. Someone deciding whether to let a
    companion hear them deserves the real answer in the sentence macOS shows, not a generic one —
    so each says what is done with the audio and where it goes.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>Saathi</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>Saathi</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>dev.saathi.Saathi</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>SaathiApp</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>__VERSION__</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>__BUILD__</string>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>13.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>Saathi</string>

    <!-- No Dock tile: Saathi is a menu-bar item plus panels. The CLI still ships beside the app
         as Contents/MacOS/saathi, which the Homebrew cask symlinks. -->
    <key>LSUIElement</key>
    <true/>

    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Saathi listens when you talk to it. In the default mode your voice is turned into text on this Mac and never leaves it.</string>

    <key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Saathi uses on-device speech recognition so it can understand you without sending your voice anywhere. Run `saathi voice` to see exactly where each part of a spoken turn goes.</string>

    <key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
    <string>Apache-2.0. Source at github.com/saathihq/saathi</string>
</dict>
</plist>
